{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgoGDS2F9kfWZ67aDyruQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The skill says to stop and get your approval before running this, even though you asked for it directly. I haven't run anything.\n\n**Command:**\n```\nbq rm -r -f -d example-project-dev:staging_old\n```\n\n**What it does:**\n- `-d` targets the dataset `staging_old` in project `example-project-dev`.\n- `-r` deletes everything inside it: all tables, views, and routines.\n- `-f` skips bq's own confirmation prompt, so nothing else will ask before the delete happens.\n\n**Can it be undone?**\n- Partly, and only for a short time. BigQuery's time-travel window (7 days by default, or whatever the dataset is set to) lets you restore individual tables that were deleted. You do that by copying a snapshot of the table as of a time before the delete.\n- That works only if the dataset still exists. Once the dataset itself is gone, you can undelete it only within the same window, and only if your project and dataset settings allow it.\n- If the dataset has a shorter time-travel window, or the tables are external or hold no data of their own, recovery is limited or impossible.\n- I haven't listed the dataset's contents. I can run `bq ls example-project-dev:staging_old` first so you can see what would go.\n\nReply \"yes\" or \"approved\" and I'll run exactly that command. If you want to see the contents first, tell me and I'll list them."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":929,"cache_read_input_tokens":19689,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":929},"output_tokens":441,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"20b82c10-2467-4dd9-91a5-ee45c8e6e1f